“Because a majority of the posterior lies within the ROPE, this provides moderate evidence that the effect is practically negligible.”

“Since most of the posterior lies above the ROPE, the effect is credibly positive and practically meaningful.”

The posterior mean effect size was 0.05 (95% CI: –0.08 to 0.16). The ROPE was defined as [–0.1, 0.1]. Approximately 74% of the posterior mass fell inside the ROPE, with 18% above and 8% below. This concentration of probability within the ROPE indicates strong evidence that the effect is practically negligible.

The posterior mean difference was 0.23 (95% CI: 0.12–0.34). Using a ROPE of [–0.1, 0.1], only 4% of the posterior fell inside the ROPE, while 95% was above it. This provides robust evidence for a positive, practically meaningful effect.

The posterior mean difference was 0.07 (95% CI: –0.04 to 0.19). With a ROPE of [–0.1, 0.1], 48% of the posterior was inside the ROPE, 41% above, and 11% below. Because the posterior is distributed across and outside the ROPE, the result is inconclusive regarding practical significance.

Justifying why not freq or BFs ------------------------

To assess whether the estimated effects were meaningfully different from zero, we used a Region of Practical Equivalence (ROPE). The ROPE specifies an interval of effect sizes considered too small to be theoretically or substantively meaningful in psychological research. For each contrast, we estimated the proportions of posterior samples falling below, inside, and above this interval, providing a direct indication of whether the effect is credibly negligible, credibly meaningful, or uncertain. This approach emphasizes interpretability on the effect scale itself and avoids overreliance on dichotomous judgments based on arbitrary thresholds.

We did not report frequentist statistics or Bayes factors because they address different inferential questions that are less aligned with our goal of assessing psychological meaningfulness. Frequentist p-values test a point null but do not convey the probability that an effect is trivial or meaningful. Bayes factors quantify relative evidence between competing models but can be heavily influenced by the choice of priors, which can be hard to interpret.do not describe where the effect lies on a continuum of practically relev. In contrast, ROPE-based inference only depends on an explicitly formulated "null hypothesis" (i.e., the ROPE itself) to check whether effects should be accepted or rejected, which provides a transparent and intuitive summary of the posterior distribution in terms of effect size magnitude, making it better suited for evaluating and interpreting meaningful effects.


Equivalence Test (from bayestestR) ----------------------
https://easystats.github.io/bayestestR/articles/region_of_practical_equivalence.html#equivalence-test

The ROPE, being a region corresponding to a “null” hypothesis, is used for the equivalence test, to test whether a parameter is significant (in the sense of important enough to be cared about). This test is usually based on the “HDI+ROPE decision rule” (J. Kruschke, 2014; J. K. Kruschke & Liddell, 2018) to check whether parameter values should be accepted or rejected against an explicitly formulated “null hypothesis” (i.e., a ROPE). In other words, it checks the percentage of Credible Interval (CI) that is the null region (the ROPE). If this percentage is sufficiently low, the null hypothesis is rejected. If this percentage is sufficiently high, the null hypothesis is accepted.

... Else, it’s unclear whether the null hypothesis should be accepted or rejected.

If the full ROPE is used (i.e., 100% of the HDI), then the null hypothesis is rejected or accepted if the percentage of the posterior within the ROPE is smaller than to 2.5% or greater than 97.5%. Desirable results are low proportions inside the ROPE (the closer to zero the better).

How to define the ROPE range?

Kruschke (2018) suggests that the ROPE could be set, by default, to a range from -0.1 to 0.1 of a standardized parameter (negligible effect size according to Cohen, 1988).

